1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,561
The Orchard Core source repository includes a Dockerfile.

2
00:00:04,561 --> 00:00:08,406
In this video, you build an image from it, run a container and

3
00:00:08,406 --> 00:00:12,250
open the site, see where the site keeps its files, and start a

4
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:14,850
database next to it with Docker Compose.

5
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:21,655
To follow along, you need Docker.

6
00:00:21,655 --> 00:00:26,914
For Windows, the documentation points to containers on the Windows Subsystem for Linux,

7
00:00:26,914 --> 00:00:31,550
and for Ubuntu or other Linux systems, to the installation of Docker Engine.

8
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:34,388
Docker runs containers from images.

9
00:00:34,388 --> 00:00:38,106
You can pull an image that Orchard Core publishes to Docker Hub, or

10
00:00:38,106 --> 00:00:42,130
build your own from the Dockerfile in the source code, the way developers

11
00:00:42,130 --> 00:00:44,320
test a pull request on their machine.

12
00:00:45,070 --> 00:00:47,762
The published image starts with one command.

13
00:00:47,762 --> 00:00:51,423
It names the container and maps a port of your machine to port

14
00:00:51,423 --> 00:00:54,330
80 inside it, where the web application listens.

15
00:00:58,530 --> 00:01:01,951
The Dockerfile sits at the root of the repository.

16
00:01:01,951 --> 00:01:06,348
Its build stage starts from an image with the .NET SDK, copies the

17
00:01:06,348 --> 00:01:09,830
source code, and publishes the CMS web application.

18
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:15,535
The runtime stages start from the ASP.NET Core runtime image, for Linux or

19
00:01:15,535 --> 00:01:19,313
for Windows, and the target operating system picks one.

20
00:01:19,313 --> 00:01:25,320
The last stage copies in only the published application, listens on port 80, and starts it.

21
00:01:26,070 --> 00:01:31,154
The .dockerignore file keeps every App_Data folder, and every bin and obj folder,

22
00:01:31,154 --> 00:01:32,498
out of the build.

23
00:01:32,498 --> 00:01:36,414
The data of a site you ran from source, and its build output,

24
00:01:36,414 --> 00:01:38,050
never enter the image.

25
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:43,541
A site built on the NuGet packages of Orchard Core can use the same two files.

26
00:01:43,541 --> 00:01:48,338
Copy the Dockerfile and .dockerignore to the root of your solution, then adjust

27
00:01:48,338 --> 00:01:51,950
the paths of the build stage to your own folder structure.

28
00:01:56,150 --> 00:02:00,254
From the root of the repository, docker build creates the image.

29
00:02:00,254 --> 00:02:05,590
The platform option sets the target operating system, which selects the Linux runtime

30
00:02:05,590 --> 00:02:08,580
stage, and the tag option names the image oc.

31
00:02:09,330 --> 00:02:12,659
Then docker run starts a container from that image.

32
00:02:12,659 --> 00:02:17,390
Its publish option maps a port of your machine to port 80 in the container.

33
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:20,404
The build stage carries a label.

34
00:02:20,404 --> 00:02:26,780
After a build, the documentation prunes the leftover intermediate images with a filter on that label.

35
00:02:31,006 --> 00:02:36,096
Browsing to the port that the container maps shows the Setup screen of Orchard Core.

36
00:02:36,096 --> 00:02:41,016
The container holds no site, so Orchard Core asks the questions that install one.

37
00:02:41,471 --> 00:02:45,903
The database type is Sqlite, the default, so nothing else has to run

38
00:02:45,903 --> 00:02:47,580
next to the container.

39
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:52,011
Let's name the site, choose the Blog recipe, and select a time zone.

40
00:02:54,664 --> 00:03:00,356
The super user needs a user name, an email address, and a password, typed twice.

41
00:03:00,356 --> 00:03:02,804
Then let's select Finish Setup.

42
00:03:03,281 --> 00:03:08,225
Orchard Core runs the recipe, creates the site inside the container, and redirects

43
00:03:08,225 --> 00:03:10,191
to the home page of the Blog.

44
00:03:14,391 --> 00:03:18,884
The site keeps its files in the App_Data folder, which is app slash

45
00:03:18,884 --> 00:03:20,854
App_Data in the container.

46
00:03:20,854 --> 00:03:25,531
Among other files, it holds the tenants file and, for each tenant, the

47
00:03:25,531 --> 00:03:29,101
Data Protection keys and, with Sqlite, the database.

48
00:03:29,851 --> 00:03:34,287
A volume mounted on App_Data keeps these files outside the container.

49
00:03:34,287 --> 00:03:38,901
A container that mounts the same volume later starts with the same site.

50
00:03:39,651 --> 00:03:46,085
Settings reach the container as environment variables, with two underscores between the levels of a name.

51
00:03:46,085 --> 00:03:50,490
The Auto Setup settings install a site without the Setup screen, and a

52
00:03:50,490 --> 00:03:52,751
separate video covers Auto Setup.

53
00:03:56,951 --> 00:04:00,293
A compose file describes several containers together.

54
00:04:00,293 --> 00:04:04,655
This one has a web service, built from the Dockerfile, and a PostgreSQL

55
00:04:04,655 --> 00:04:06,411
service for the database.

56
00:04:07,161 --> 00:04:11,051
The web service builds from the Dockerfile in the current folder, maps a

57
00:04:11,051 --> 00:04:14,791
port of your machine to port 80, and depends on the database service.

58
00:04:15,541 --> 00:04:20,078
The database service sets its user, its database name, and its password.

59
00:04:20,078 --> 00:04:24,790
Compose reads the password from an environment variable of your own, so the

60
00:04:24,790 --> 00:04:26,361
file holds no secret.

61
00:04:27,111 --> 00:04:33,704
Set the password variable, run docker-compose build, and then docker-compose up to start both containers.

62
00:04:33,704 --> 00:04:37,861
Open the mapped port in a browser, and the Setup screen appears.

63
00:04:38,611 --> 00:04:43,913
On the Setup screen, choose Postgres as the type of database, and enter a connection string.

64
00:04:43,913 --> 00:04:47,917
Its server is the name of the database service, and the other values

65
00:04:47,917 --> 00:04:50,081
come from that service's settings.

66
00:04:52,081 --> 00:04:56,956
The Dockerfile builds Orchard Core into an image, a container serves it, the

67
00:04:56,956 --> 00:05:01,355
App_Data folder holds its state, and Docker Compose adds a database.

68
00:05:01,355 --> 00:05:02,901
Thanks for watching.

